¶
'''tide-gate'''<sup>2</sup> (tīd′gāt), ''n.'' [< ''tide''<sup>1</sup> + ''gate''<sup>2</sup>.] 1†. Tideway; stream.

{{smaller block|Some visible apparent tokens remaine of a haven, . . . though now it be graveld up, and the streame or ''tydegate'' turned another way.
{{right|''Nashe'', Lenten Stuffe (Harl. Misc., VI. 150). (''Davies.'')}}}}

2. ''Naut.'', a narrow place where the tide runs with great velocity.

'''tide-harbor''' (tīd′här″bor), ''n.'' Same as ''tidal harbor'' (which see, under ''tidal'').

'''tide-land''' (tīd′land), ''n.'' Such land as is affected by the tide; land which is alternately covered and left dry by the ordinary flux and reflux of the tides.—'''Tide-land spruce.''' {{smaller|See ''spruce''<sup>3</sup>.}}

'''tideless''' (tīd′les), ''a.'' [< ''tide''<sup>1</sup> + ''-less''.] Without ebb or flow.

{{smaller block|There is a considerable fresh water volume debouching into a ''tideless'' sea or lake.
{{right|''Jour. Franklin Inst.'', CXXV. 306.}}}}

'''tide-lock''' (tīd′lok), ''n.'' A lock situated between the tide-water of a harbor or river and an inclosed basin when their levels vary. {{smaller|It has two pairs of double gates, by which vessels can pass either way at all times of the tide. Also called ''guard-lock''.}}

'''tidely†''' (tīd′li), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''tidely'', ''tydely'', < AS. ''tīdlīce'' (= D. ''tijdelijk'' = G. ''zeitlich''), timely, seasonably, < ''tīdlīc'' (= D. ''tijdelijk'' = G. ''zeitlich''), timely, seasonable, < ''tīd'', time, tide: see ''tide''<sup>1</sup> and ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] 1. Seasonably; opportunely; suitably; fitly.

{{smaller block|But [he] tok to him ''tidely'' trewe cunsayl euere.
{{right|''William of Palerne'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 5482.}}}}

{{smaller block|Item, Sir, if my Maister of the Rolles be not come, I trust to God to com ''tydely'' i now, as for the traversys.
{{right|''Paston Letters'', I. 528.}}}}

