{{smaller block|These big fellows [weakfish] are designated as ''tide-runners.''
{{right|''Sportsman's Gazetteer,'' p. 244.}}}}

'''tides-man''' (tīdz′man), ''n.'' 1. One who is employed only during certain states of the tide.—2. A tidewaiter.

'''tide-table''' (tīd′tā″bl), ''n.'' A table showing the time of high water at any place, or at different places, for each day throughout the year.

'''tidewaiter''' (tīd′wā″tėr), ''n.'' One of a class of custom-house officers whose business it is to await the arrival of ships, and to see that while in port the customs regulations as to the landing and shipping of goods are observed, and the revenue laws are not violated.

{{smaller block|If he misses a pair of colours, or a ''tide-waiter's'' place, he has no remedy but the highway.
{{right|''Swift,'' Advice to Servants (Waiting-Maid).}}}}

{{smaller block|The father of the Custom-House — the patriarch not only of this little squad of officials, but, I am bold to say, of the respectable body of ''tide-waiters'' all over the United States — was a certain permanent Inspector.
{{right|''Hawthorne,'' Scarlet Letter, Int., p. 17.}}}}

'''tide-water''' (tīd′wâ″tėr), ''n.'' Water affected by the ordinary ebb and flow of the tide.—'''Tide-water region''', {{smaller|the low plain of eastern Virginia, extending from the Atlantic coast westward about 100 miles.}}

'''tide-wave''' (tīd′wāv), ''n.'' A tidal wave (which see, under ''tidal'').

