3. To fasten by tightening and knotting the strings of: as, to ''tie'' a shoe or a bonnet.

{{smaller block|Drawer, ''tie'' my shoe, prithee; the new knot, as thou seest this.
{{right|''Dekker and Webster'', Northward Ho, i. 2.}}}}

4. To form by looping and interlacing; knit: as, to ''tie'' a knot.

{{smaller block|Again the hawthorn shall supply
The garlands you delight to ''tie''.
{{right|''Scott'', Marmion, i., Int.}}}}

5. To bind or unite securely; specifically, to unite in marriage (colloq. in this use).

{{smaller block|And doe they not knowe that a Tragedie is ''tied'' to the lawes of Poesie, and not of Historie?
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Apol. for Poetrie.}}}}

{{smaller block|In bond of virtuous love together ''tied''.
{{right|''Fairfax.''}}}}

{{smaller block|I heartily desire this courtesy,
And would not be denied, to wait upon you
This day, to see you ''tied'', then no more trouble you.
{{right|''Fletcher'', Wildgoose Chase, iv. 1.}}}}

6. To bind, restrict, limit, or confine; hold or restrain, as by authority or moral influence.

{{smaller block|Herewith hir swelling sobbes
Did ''tie'' hir tong from talke.
{{right|''Gascoigne'', Philomene (Steele Glas, etc., ed. Arber, p. 99).}}}}

{{smaller block|I see you are ''tied'' to no particular employment.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Scornful Lady, i. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|Do they think to bind me to live chaste, sober, and temperately all days of my life? they may as soon ''tie'' an Englishman to live so.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Knight of Malta, v. 1.}}}}

